James inquired about the global methane action report from 2011 that led to the establishment of the Climate and Clean Air Coalition (CCAC). I identified the relevant document as the "Integrated Assessment of Black Carbon and Tropospheric Ozone," published by UNEP and WMO. This report emphasized the significant effects of short-lived climate pollutants (SLCPs), including methane, on climate change and public health. It served as a scientific basis for launching the CCAC, which aims to reduce SLCPs for better climate protection and improved air quality.
